MAME ctlsock: a KEY field spec may carry its default-assignment token

WHY

The key engine resolves `KEY <0|1> <port> <field>` by the field's display
name. Drivers that declare PORT_CODE without PORT_NAME get MAME's generic
"Unnamed Key" for every such field — the Apollo keyboard (apollo_kbd.cpp)
has three on :kbd:keyboard1: Backspace, Tab and Return — and a name-only
lookup returns the first, so every Enter the daemon sent was a Backspace
(domainos wave, 2026-09-09; measured with the apollo_kbd LOG1 trace).

WHAT

A spec of the form `<name>#KEYCODE_<X>` also requires the field's default
assignment to be that token — the same token KEYDUMP already prints as its
third column, so scripts/dev/mame-keymap.py can emit it whenever a name is
shared inside one port. Specs without `#KEYCODE_` behave exactly as before.
No save items change; the savestate signature is untouched. Applies after
mame-ctlsock.patch.

--- a/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
@@ -1879,11 +1879,34 @@
 		return found;
 	}
 
-	static ioport_field *find_field(ioport_port *port, std::string const &name)
+	// A field spec is the field's display name, optionally followed by
+	// '#' and its default-assignment token (KEYDUMP's third column):
+	// "Unnamed Key#KEYCODE_ENTER". Drivers that omit PORT_NAME on
+	// Backspace/Tab/Return (the Apollo keyboard, domainos wave 2026-09-09)
+	// give all three the same "Unnamed Key" name, and a name-only lookup
+	// returned the first of them -- Backspace -- for every Enter the daemon
+	// sent. With the token the match is exact; without it, unchanged.
+	ioport_field *find_field(ioport_port *port, std::string const &spec) const
 	{
+		std::string name = spec, tok;
+		size_t const hash = spec.rfind('#');
+		if (hash != std::string::npos && spec.compare(hash + 1, 8, "KEYCODE_") == 0)
+		{
+			name = spec.substr(0, hash);
+			tok = spec.substr(hash + 1);
+		}
 		for (ioport_field &f : port->fields())
-			if (f.name() == name)
-				return &f;
+		{
+			if (f.name() != name)
+				continue;
+			if (!tok.empty())
+			{
+				input_seq const &dseq = f.defseq();
+				if (dseq.length() == 0 || m_machine.input().code_to_token(dseq[0]) != tok)
+					continue;
+			}
+			return &f;
+		}
 		return nullptr;
 	}
 
